// Practice quiz: Reading: logging in Spring
// Minimum release, November 2026: author-checked, no independent review. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: spring-boot/integration-and-observability/logging-in-spring

::o1 default level::What is the default root logging level in Spring Boot? {
=INFO#Correct. DEBUG and TRACE are filtered by default.
~DEBUG#Not quite. That is more detailed than the default.
~ERROR#Not quite. INFO and WARN also appear.
~TRACE#Not quite. That is the most detailed.
}

::o1 placeholder::Why write log.debug("rows \{\}", n) rather than joining strings? {
=The message is only built if the level is enabled#Correct. That avoids needless work.
~Joining strings does not compile#Not quite. It compiles.
~Placeholders encrypt the value#Not quite. They do not.
~It makes it an ERROR#Not quite. The method sets the level.
}

::o1 package level::Which property sets DEBUG for one package? {
=logging.level.com.acme\=debug#Correct. The logger name is part of the key.
~server.debug\=com.acme#Not quite. That is not a logging property.
~spring.debug.package#Not quite. That is not a property.
~logging.file.name#Not quite. That sets the file name.
}

::o2 reading a line::In a default console log line, what follows the level and the process id? {
=The application and thread name, then the logger and message#Correct. These identify the source of the line.
~The SQL statement#Not quite. Only SQL logging shows that.
~The password#Not quite. It must never be there.
~The JVM version#Not quite. That is not on each line.
}

::o3 no secrets::Which of these should never appear in a log? {
=A bearer token#Correct. It works as a credential.
~A booking id#Not quite. Plain ids are usually fine.
~A status code#Not quite. That is fine.
~A duration in milliseconds#Not quite. That is fine.
}

